WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:07.069
In 1999 a tiny little independent film called
The Blair Witch Project was released.

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:09.790
Zachary: And it went on to be this smash hit.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.660
It made $250 million dollars worldwide.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.269
It also got rave reviews.

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:15.719
Zachary: Even from Roger Ebert.

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:17.970
He called it “an extraordinarily effective
horror film”

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.890
It was actually one of the most successful
independent films ever made.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:27.460
Riding on this success its sequel, Book of
Shadows: Blair Witch II was released just

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.460
a year later.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.760
Z: It was universally hated by every single
critic.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.680
Someone called it "a club footed vomit launch
of teen horror cliches"

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.219
It was bad.

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:39.870
Really bad.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:51.460
And it got us wondering what are the best
and worst sequels of the past 20 years?

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.511
Anytime you google search a film you’ll
immediately be presented with it’s metacritic

00:00:55.511 --> 00:00:56.511
rating.

00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:01.090
Z: Basically you have all these movie critics
all around the united states and they use

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.390
different systems to review movies.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.640
Metacritic normalizes these rankings on a
1-100 scale, putting more weight on critics

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.640
with more clout.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.640
Zachary, my colleague here.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:10.640
Metacritic normalizes these rankings on a
1-100 scale, putting more weight on critics

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:11.640
with more clout.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.970
Zachary, my colleague here.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:17.890
Was given a data set of 13,000 films from
Metacritic and found every movie with a sequel

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.049
since 1996.

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:22.090
532 films total.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:27.470
Z: What we wanted to know was what were the
films that were really good the first time

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.040
around and really crappy the second time around.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.240
In other words, the biggest disappointments.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.390
That’s where films like Book of Shadows:
Blair Witch 2 comes in.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.231
Z: So the original got a score of 81 points.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:40.231
The sequel got a score of 15 points.

00:01:40.231 --> 00:01:43.220
CLIP FROM MOVIE: You know if you don’t believe
in the Blair Witch then why did you bother

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.220
to come?

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.049
I thought the movie was cool
Zachary: The sequel got a score of 15 points.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:50.530
The 66 points less.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.700
Or take Crouching Tiger Hidden Dragon, the
first Mandarin language film nominated for

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.110
Best Picture.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.810
It went on to win Best Foreign Language film.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:03.190
16 years later Netflix released a sequel,
in English.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:06.460
The orginal got a score of 93.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.460
The sequel?

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.080
Z: So unfortunately it fared much worse.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.459
CLIP FROM MOVIE: Too much blazing flame!

00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:12.900
It got 47.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:19.150
Z: So on the flip side you have films that
did really poorly the first time around and

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.629
somehow miraculously did better when they
were made into a sequel.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:26.940
Z: This is a really small contingent of all
the sequels we looked at.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.280
Less than 25 of the 532 films were improvements.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.290
Outside of movies like The Dark Knight and
Kill Bill vol 2 most of these movies were

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.989
tremendously bad to begin with.

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:43.810
CLIP: Awe good boy!

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.880
See I knew you could do it if you put your
mind to it.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:46.880
Z: Like for instance Garfield got a score
of 27.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:47.930
It’s not that hard to improve on that.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.090
Like Garfield Part 2 got 37 which is still
pretty bad.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.319
Z: The worst ones fall into 1 of 3 categories.

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:59.879
It’s either a horror, comedy, or action
film.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:05.530
So I wanted to see if that’s a bigger indication
of how critics rate those movies.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:13.060
Z: So I looked at a randomized selection of
200 films from every single genre.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.890
And then averaged out the score for each genre.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:19.980
This chart reveals that the genres with the
worst sequels also fair pretty badly amongst

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.599
critics in general.

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:25.099
Despite all of this, Hollywood loves making
them.

00:03:25.099 --> 00:03:30.380
So much so that this year alone an unprecedented
37 sequels will be released.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.380
The reason?

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.380
Money.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.850
Z: The average sequel makes 8x the average
original release.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:42.049
That’s $160,000,000 dollars over the $20,000,000
original.

00:03:42.049 --> 00:03:47.830
Grown Ups, the Adam Sandler movie which was
rated an abysmal 30% on metacritic received

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:50.549
270 million dollars worldwide.

00:03:50.549 --> 00:03:56.930
Its sequel, which somehow dropped 11 points
still managed 247 million dollars worldwide.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.090
It was a box office hit.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.790
Audiences had no reason to give Grown Ups
2 a chance.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.440
And yet they did.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:09.540
Roger Ebert famously said “No movie executive
was fired because he greenlit a sequel....

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:15.439
But movie critics despair of sequels as betraying
a lack of imagination and originality."

00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:35.120
If you have any doubts about how true that
is give Grown Ups 2 a whirl.

